# Grandma Ruth 120 · ANGLE: family secret revealed at a funeral (mother secretly ate Christmas dinner at a soup kitchen for decades)

sc("""Sweetheart, every Christmas Day of my whole life, at three o'clock, right after dinner, my mother put on her oldest coat and a scarf with holes in it, and left the house. She said she was helping at the church. She came home at dark. For thirty-five years. Nobody ever asked her where she really went. And then, at her funeral in nineteen eighty-nine, the back of the church filled up with strangers.""",
   "woman in old coat leaving house in snow", "AT THREE O'CLOCK")
sc("""Tonight I'm going to tell you who those strangers were, where my mother really went every Christmas afternoon, and the reason she never told a soul, not even my father. Stay with me, honey, because it goes back to a soup line in the winter of nineteen thirty-two, when she was a little girl.""",
   "1930s Depression soup line in winter", "A SOUP LINE IN 1932")

ch("Three o'clock")
sc("""Christmas at our house on Elm Street was always the same, honey. Church in the morning. Presents. Dinner at one, ham and sweet potatoes and my mother's pie. And then, at three o'clock, while my father fell asleep in his chair and Danny and I played with our new things, my mother would go to the hall closet and take out that old brown coat. The one with the torn pocket she'd never fixed.""",
   "1950s family Christmas dinner table", "THE OLD BROWN COAT")
sc("""She had a good coat, sweetheart. A navy wool one my father bought her. But on Christmas afternoon, it was always the brown one, and an old gray scarf with moth holes, and no lipstick. I asked her once, when I was about ten, why she wore the ugly coat. And she said, because it's warm, Ruthie, and the church hall is drafty. And she kissed my head and went out the door. In a minute I'll tell you what I saw from the window.""",
   "girl watching mother from window", "WHY THE UGLY COAT?")
sc("""I watched her walk down Elm Street, honey, in the snow. But she didn't turn left, toward First Lutheran. She turned right, toward downtown. I thought maybe she was going to visit a sick lady. I didn't think much more about it. Children don't. And every year after that, three o'clock, the brown coat, the gray scarf, and home after dark, cold, with her cheeks pink, very quiet.""",
   "woman walking down snowy street toward downtown", "SHE TURNED RIGHT")

sc("""Danny had a theory, honey. When he was twelve, he decided our mother had a secret boyfriend. He told me so in a whisper under the covers, very dramatic. I said, Danny, she's wearing the ugliest coat in Ohio. Who's she going to meet in that? He said, that's the disguise, Ruthie. We laughed so hard my father banged on the wall. I'll tell you how close to the truth Danny actually was in a minute.""",
   "two children whispering under blanket at night", "A SECRET BOYFRIEND?")
ch("Growing up")
sc("""When I grew up and married Harold, honey, we had Christmas at my parents' house every year. And every year, at three o'clock, my mother put on that brown coat. Harold asked about it once. My father just shook his head and said, Dorothy has her church work, and I've learned not to ask. My father was a patient man. He trusted her completely. And she never explained. Not once, in thirty-five years.""",
   "family gathered at grandparents house 1970s Christmas", "I'VE LEARNED NOT TO ASK")
sc("""If Grandma's story is touching your heart, tap the like button for me, sweetheart. It helps more people find these stories. My father passed in nineteen eighty-four. And that first Christmas without him, I thought surely she'd stay home. She was sixty-four, and grieving, and it was nine degrees outside. But at three o'clock, she stood up from the table. And I said, Mother, please, not today. And she said, especially today, Ruthie. And she went.""",
   "elderly widow putting on old coat at door", "ESPECIALLY TODAY")

ch("The funeral")
sc("""My mother passed in March of nineteen eighty-nine, honey. Her heart, in her sleep, in her own bed. She was sixty-nine. The funeral was at First Lutheran, and we expected maybe sixty people, family, church ladies, neighbors from Elm Street. And it was all those people. But as the service was starting, the back doors kept opening. And men kept coming in. One at a time. And sitting in the back pews.""",
   "church funeral with people in pews", "THE BACK DOORS KEPT OPENING")
sc("""They were old men, mostly, sweetheart, and some younger. In worn-out coats, and work boots, and knit caps they took off and held in their hands. Some of them hadn't shaved. One had a cane made from a broom handle. They didn't know anybody. They didn't sit with anybody. They filled the last four pews, and they stood along the back wall. By the time the pastor started, there were more than forty of them.""",
   "poor men in worn coats in back of church", "MORE THAN FORTY")
sc("""The church ladies were whispering, honey. Who are they? Are they in the right church? And I didn't know either. I sat in the front pew with Harold and Danny, and I kept turning around to look. They sat through the whole service, very quiet, very respectful. Some of them were crying. And when it was over, they came up the aisle, one by one, to the casket. Stay with me, honey.""",
   "men walking up church aisle to casket", "ONE BY ONE")

sc("""Danny leaned over to me in the pew and whispered, Ruthie, I think I was wrong about the boyfriend. And I'm ashamed to say I laughed, at my own mother's funeral, and then I cried, because I didn't know what any of it meant. Harold squeezed my hand. He said, quietly, Ruthie, I think we're about to find out where she went every Christmas. He always was the smart one.""",
   "man whispering to sister in church pew", "WE'RE ABOUT TO FIND OUT")
ch("Eddie")
sc("""So far, the brown coat, three o'clock every Christmas, the turn to the right, and forty strangers at my mother's funeral. The last one up the aisle was a tall, thin man, about seventy, with a gray beard and a cane. He stood by the casket a long time. Then he came over to the front pew, and he looked at me, and he said, are you Ruthie? And I said, yes. Who are you?""",
   "old man with cane and beard by casket", "ARE YOU RUTHIE?")
sc("""He said, my name is Eddie, ma'am. Your mother ate Christmas dinner with me every year for thirty-one years. At the St. Vincent's soup kitchen on Fourth Street. And I just stared at him, honey. And he said, she never told you, did she? She said she wouldn't. She said if anybody knew she came, they'd think she was there to do charity. And she didn't want us to feel like charity.""",
   "old soup kitchen hall with long tables", "ST. VINCENT'S")

ch("What she did")
sc("""Eddie told me everything, honey, standing by my mother's casket. She came every Christmas at three-thirty, when the volunteers had gone home and the hall was emptying out. In her brown coat and gray scarf. She'd go through the line, and get a plate, just like everybody else. And she'd sit down at a table with whoever was sitting alone. And she'd eat with them, and talk with them, for two hours.""",
   "woman in brown coat sitting with lonely man at table", "WHOEVER WAS ALONE")
sc("""She knew all their names, sweetheart. She asked about their families, their old jobs, where they grew up. She remembered from year to year. She brought a pie, every year, in a plain tin, and set it on the dessert table without a word. Eddie said, the volunteers were kind. But they stood behind the counter. Your mother sat down at the table. She ate what we ate. That's why we came today.""",
   "pie in plain tin on soup kitchen table", "SHE SAT WITH US")
sc("""Eddie said, the first year I met her, nineteen fifty-eight, I'd lost my job and my wife. I was going to end it that night, ma'am. I'd decided. And this lady in a brown coat sat down across from me and asked me where I learned to fix engines, because I had grease under my nails. We talked till dark. And I went home. And I came back the next year, because I wanted to see her again.""",
   "man and woman talking across soup kitchen table", "I WENT HOME")

sc("""He said, she came to my wedding, ma'am, in nineteen sixty-four. I met a good woman at the soup kitchen, a volunteer, and we got married, and your mother came, in that same brown coat. She sat in the back. She brought a pie. Then he took a little photograph out of his pocket, very creased, and showed me. A wedding couple on church steps, and in the corner, half cut off, a woman in a brown coat, holding a pie tin.""",
   "creased old wedding photo on church steps", "SHE BROUGHT A PIE")
ch("Nineteen thirty-two")
sc("""But the why, honey, I didn't learn until after the funeral. When I cleaned out my mother's dresser, I found an old Christmas card, from nineteen thirty-two, very worn, with a note inside in her handwriting from much later. It said, the Christmas I was twelve, Papa had lost his job at the rubber plant, and we had nothing. So we went to the soup line on Fourth Street. Papa wouldn't lift his head. He was so ashamed.""",
   "worn 1930s Christmas card with note", "PAPA WOULDN'T LIFT HIS HEAD")
sc("""It said, and then a lady in a fur coat came in and stood behind the counter and handed us our plates, and said, Merry Christmas, you poor things. And Papa stopped eating. And then a different lady, in an old coat like ours, sat down at our table. She ate the same soup. She asked Papa about his work and told him he had clever hands. And Papa lifted his head, and he laughed. I hadn't heard him laugh in a year.""",
   "1930s father laughing at soup kitchen table", "PAPA LAUGHED")
sc("""And at the bottom, my mother had written, I promised God that night, if we ever had enough, I would be the lady in the old coat. Never the lady in the fur. I sat on the floor of her bedroom, honey, holding that card. And I understood the brown coat. And the torn pocket she never fixed. And especially today. She'd been keeping that promise for thirty-five years. And she never told a soul.""",
   "woman sitting on floor holding old card crying", "THE OLD COAT")

ch("The coat")
sc("""That Christmas, nineteen eighty-nine, at three o'clock, I stood up from the table, sweetheart. I went to my own closet, and I took out my mother's brown coat, which I'd kept. And her gray scarf. And Harold looked at me, and he didn't ask. He just went and got his own oldest jacket. And we walked down to St. Vincent's together. And Eddie was there, at a table by the window. He'd saved two seats.""",
   "couple entering soup kitchen in old coats", "HE SAVED TWO SEATS")
sc("""Harold and I went every Christmas afternoon after that, honey, until he passed. Then I went with Susan, then with my grandchildren. Eddie came until nineteen ninety-six. St. Vincent's is a different building now, bigger and brighter. But every Christmas Day, at three-thirty, somebody from our family walks in wearing an old coat, gets a plate, and sits down with whoever is sitting alone. My granddaughter Lily goes now. She wears the gray scarf.""",
   "young woman in gray scarf at soup kitchen table", "SHE WEARS THE SCARF")

ch("Grandma's lesson")
sc("""Here's what I want you to remember, honey. Don't stand behind the counter. Sit down at the table. That's the difference between charity and love. Tell me in the comments, sweetheart, did someone in your family do something kind in secret? There are more of my family's stories in the playlist right there on your screen. Good night. Merry Christmas.""",
   "old brown coat and gray scarf on hook", "SIT DOWN AT THE TABLE")

